this is vladivostok the largest russian  city on the pacific ocean  vladivostok is home to a number of  unique attractions including one of the  country's only two funiculars  commissioned as part of a 1959 plan to  build soviet san francisco  impressive bridges built for the 2012  apex summit  and one of the world's largest aquariums  but perhaps its most unusual attraction  is something you won't find listed on  any travel guide  repair korea an exclusively north korean  contractor specializing in home repair  and renovation  its website translated here to english  acknowledges your skepticism  some people think you shouldn't invite  koreans to repair your apartment  but reassures you that quote unlike  spoiled russian specialists koreans work  extremely professionally accurately and  in full compliance with the world  quality standards  but don't assume north korean builders  are confined to any one construction  niche  others helped build the world cup  stadium in st petersburg and apartment  buildings in moscow  neither do they work only in russia  after the u.s state department commended  the country of kuwait for cutting ties  with north korea kuwait responded with  confusion saying there are no plans to  expel north korean laborers and kuwait  has never done so  the regime collects the vast majority of  these wages while recipient nations get  access to cheap hard-working labor  this trade is often cited as proof that  sanctions don't work it seems there's  always a way around them  after the un demanded that countries  expel north korean workers in 2017 for  instance russia simply reissued them  tourist and student visas  the country is also responsible for  countless hacking campaigns ransomware  attacks the production of counterfeits  and a dozen other illicit odd jobs  but in fact the opposite  the extreme length that goes to evade  sanctions even when the payoff is  relatively small suggests they do have a  powerful effect  and yet there hasn't been any meaningful  diplomatic progress in  decades so why do sanctions bring iran  to the negotiating table but not north  korea  sponsored by curiosity stream and nebula  get both and watch the bonus video that  accompanies this one for just 15 bucks a  year  north korea has sought nuclear weapons  with varying degrees of urgency for its  entire existence  in 1959 just six years after the end of  the korean war the soviet union agreed  to build it a reactor though not without  conditions  the soviets pressured them to sign the  non-proliferation treaty which they  finally did in 1985  but inconsistencies emerged almost  immediately  after signing the treaty countries are  required to submit an initial report in  which they declare how much nuclear  material is already in their possession  north korea waited seven years to do so  and international inspectors soon  noticed a discrepancy in the amount of  declared plutonium  it then refused to allow further  inspections withdrew from the treaty and  even neglected its binding commitments  but this was only the first of many  broken promises to come  in 1994 after four months of  negotiations the u.s agreed to give it  two light water reactors and 500 000  tons of fuel oil annually in exchange  for the freezing of its weapons program  then north korea was caught secretly  enriching uranium and according to the  us even admitted to it  a new round of negotiations began in  2003 which appeared to reach a  breakthrough  nuclear facilities were dismantled and  the country was removed from america's  list of state sponsors of terrorism  predictably north korea announced it  would restart its nuclear program  several years later  at this point no doubt remained about  its true intentions dishonesty had been  demonstrated time and time and time  again  perhaps even more worrisome was its long  history of sporadic and dangerous  behavior  this includes multiple attempted  assassinations of the south korean  president airplane bombs the 1968  capture of an american ship and firing  at both military and civilian planes  and when the carrot fails conventional  wisdom would suggest it's time for the  stick  sanctions were the only option left it  seemed for a country that couldn't be  trusted  over time they expanded from covering  military equipment to travel luxury  goods raw materials and even food  severely restricting even basic  humanitarian aid  but just as negotiation appeared less  and less effective over time sanctions  have now been around long enough to  identify a pattern  they don't work  the problem is not contrary to popular  thinking that sanctions are easily  evaded in 2017 and 18 a decline in trade  led to a 3.5 and 4.1 percent decrease in  its gdp  exports declined by 90 percent in 2018.  of course neither are they perfectly  effective  far from it  there will always be black markets and  someone else with a vested interest in  north korea's continued existence  china for instance supplies its neighbor  with just enough coal oil and food to  prevent its collapse the vacuum of which  would soon be filled by an american ally  this measly income is akin to an  unemployed person recycling bottles and  cans it's not nothing but pretty close  at the scale of a nation  to put things in perspective north  korea's gdp is half that of vermont's  the u.s state with the smallest economy  yet its population is 40 times larger  the problem is that sanctions don't work  as intended they clearly haven't ended  its nuclear program or put an end to  missile tests  the first reason is that the wrong  people are affected  the only group whose suffering might  induce change are elites yet the burden  of sanctions is mostly felt by the  middle class and below  as inflation increases average  working-class households struggle to  maintain the same standard of living  high sanction-driven prices mean that 97  of afghanistan will fall below the  poverty line this year according to a  u.n estimate  when the rich and powerful are affected  they can simply cut back on social  programs to compensate  now you might argue this is how things  are supposed to work  the civilian population puts pressure on  the government and the threat of losing  power encourages it to change course  but this effect is strongest in  democracies which sanctioned countries  are less likely to be  north korea on the other hand goes well  beyond less democratic it's the textbook  example of a country unlikely to  experience political revolution  insulated from the rest of the world in  virtually every respect it can easily  control the narrative blaming higher  prices and lower quality of life on the  enemy  if anything sanctions have the opposite  effect  just as residents of yugoslavia blamed  the un not their own government in the  90s they instill a unifying patriotism  among north koreans  again the net effect of sanctions for  its leaders is unquestionably negative  they lead to malnutrition death disease  population decline and ultimately  economic failure  after the collapse of the soviet union  the regime's closest ally between 600  000 and 1 million koreans died of hunger  while sanctions were not the direct  cause they certainly didn't help  even if you believe these deaths mean  nothing to kim jong-un the axis of evil  label diminishes the country's  legitimacy it desperately wants to be  treated like a normal country  this is evidenced according to many  experts by the continuous cycle of  missile tests which are followed by  demands for negotiation  despite having long ago demonstrated its  nuclear capabilities it's nevertheless  discontent with the status quo  so we're seemingly back to square one  if it wants sanctions lifted why doesn't  it strike a deal  to answer this question try putting  yourself no matter how unnatural it may  feel in the shoes of kim jong-un whose  number one fear is regime change  this deeply held fear is not stalinist  paranoia or an archaic leftover of the  soviet era  kim jong-un has every legitimate reason  to worry  after all none of this is theoretical  libya acceded to u.s demands halting the  development of its nuclear program in  2003.  eight years later the dead body of  muammar gaddafi was on public display  just in case the lesson that an autocrat  should never surrender his weapons of  mass destruction wasn't made clear  enough american officials have since  made repeated unequivocal threats that  the same thing could happen to kim  jong-un  in the lead-up to the 2018 singapore  summit for instance president trump  warned that his north korean counterpart  would suffer the same fate as gaddafi if  a deal wasn't reached  u.s south korean and japanese forces  regularly conduct mock decapitation  drills on its doorstep  and in 2006 former secretary of defense  william perry published an op-ed titled  if necessary strike and destroy  none of this obviously absolves north  korea of any responsibility  its negotiating behavior has ranged from  merely skittish to downright deceitful  but in truth the us has also given it  plenty of genuine reason for skepticism  what the simplistic version of events  suggesting north korea never intended on  following through on its promises from  earlier misses is the lack of american  political continuity  the 1994 agreed framework when north  korea was caught secretly enriching  uranium for example is remembered as a  failure while its actions certainly  violated the spirit of the agreement it  did suspend production of plutonium as  promised which was a significant step in  the right direction  also missing is that the clinton  administration completed a review of u.s  policy on september 15 1999  recommending quote a new comprehensive  and integrated approach to negotiations  but that radical new approach never came  to fruition because george bush won the  presidency 14 months later  bush began his own policy review which  took another year and ended by  effectively suspending the agreed  framework  now the u.s had every right to withdraw  from its side of the agreement after all  north korea had clearly violated it  first  but in doing so it let north korea off  the hook for everything allowing it to  resume production of plutonium  not only did america willfully lose the  little bit of progress it had made it  also permanently diminished the very  currency of negotiations  trust  again blame lies squarely in north  korea's court but one can easily see how  america's actions would inspire less  than supreme confidence in pyongyang  in addition to the lack of political  continuity which leads countries around  the world to doubt america's long-term  commitments and think they can simply  wait it out for a new administration  there's also the issue of funding  presidents can negotiate but only  congress controls the purse  when the two branches disagree  commitments made by the president may be  broken  the other country won't care whose exact  fault it is only that the promise was  broken  another neglected aspect of the agreed  framework were the delays  angry that the u.s was quote rewarding  the enemy with fuel oil and two light  water reactors which cannot be used to  build nuclear weapons congress withheld  funding which led north korea to doubt  its sincerity  a lot has changed in the last 30 some  years  aside from a few brief pauses here and  there north korea has continued  improving its capabilities from  possessing one or two bombs to missile  delivery hydrogen bombs ballistic  missiles and most recently long-range  road mobile ballistic missiles capable  of reaching anywhere on the u.s mainland  and yet the u.s still demands the exact  same thing it did in 1990  complete verifiable and irreversible  denuclearization  its bottom line is that north korea  abandoned 30 years of development  economic sacrifices and likely the only  thing keeping kim jong-un alive  if this was ever a realistic demand it  stopped being one decades ago  each successive american president  arrives fresh in the white house  believing they can be the one to finally  resolve the crisis once and for all  but they soon discover that there are  only two possible pathways  either be forever known as the one to  accept that it's too late that north  korea is and will remain a nuclear power  that there's nothing the u.s can do  about it and risk being perceived as  soft on the enemy of all enemies  or accept nothing less than the only  thing kim jong-un won't ever agree to  complete denuclearization  setting themselves up to fail from the  very beginning  if the goal is merely to punish the  united states has unquestionably  succeeded but more likely having an  effect has been confused with  effectiveness  the irony is that by refusing to accept  even one percent risk america must now  accept much more than one percent risk  the world's most powerful country cannot  cope with the fact that the one country  beyond its control has an economy the  size of mozambique and a leader whose  most discernible personality trait is a  passion for swiss cheese  so accustomed to having the final say  the ability to kill any leader of any  country at any time  the u.s is almost perfectly ill-suited  to deal with a country where such  threats are exactly the cause of angst  and therefore bad behavior in the first  place  so what's the solution and is north  korea an exception or are economic  sanctions vastly overrated  both these questions are the subject of  the bonus video that accompanies this  one on nebula the creator-owned  streaming platform  by signing up for nebula you get access  to a dozen other polymatter bonus videos  and extended content exclusive originals  and for the same low price curiosity  stream  curiosity stream is home to great  documentaries on history science and  technology  another country that struggled under the  weight of u.s sanctions is cuba but the  lack of tourist has turned its natural  environment into an untouched experiment  this documentary explores that dilemma  can cuba keep its beaches pristine or  will it eventually succumb to financial  pressure  click the link on screen right now to  get both nebula and curiosity stream for  just 15 bucks a year and go watch the  bonus video over on nebula
 

